1
00:00:00,120 --> 00:00:03,185
When a tourist from Thailand pays
a Bali stall with QRIS,

2
00:00:03,185 --> 00:00:05,458
the money is never turned into US dollars.

3
00:00:05,458 --> 00:00:09,000
Most people would guess it gets
converted somewhere in the middle.

4
00:00:10,067 --> 00:00:13,627
The Thai app sees that the code is Indonesian
and sends the payment to Thailand's

5
00:00:13,687 --> 00:00:14,007
switch.

6
00:00:15,047 --> 00:00:17,447
That switch passes it across
to Indonesia's switch,

7
00:00:17,827 --> 00:00:20,807
which takes it to the stall's bank
down the same road as before.

8
00:00:21,933 --> 00:00:26,413
Behind the scenes, a few appointed banks
in each country swap rupiah and

9
00:00:26,513 --> 00:00:27,813
baht with each other directly.

10
00:00:28,733 --> 00:00:31,293
That final handover between
banks is called settlement,

11
00:00:31,813 --> 00:00:34,013
and here it happens in the
two local currencies.
